start_ebae6d9e		Balancing Commitments and Wellbeing: Scenario Start	game/script.rpy	43	"[what]"
start_a3790c2c		Monday, 7:42 PM.	game/script.rpy	45	"[what]"
start_cbe60ae5		Your laptop is open. Three browser tabs are displaying deadlines, your phone is vibrating, and a mug of tea has been sitting untouched long enough to qualify as a historical artefact.	game/script.rpy	47	"[what]"
start_dce1bf64	y	Right. Let's assess the situation rationally.	game/script.rpy	49	y "[what]"
start_b8488f13	y	I have a university assignment due Wednesday. I have a work deadline on Thursday. And I promised the charity that I would review a document before Friday's meeting.	game/script.rpy	51	y "[what]"
start_71b7a4a1	y	Three perfectly reasonable commitments. Individually manageable. Collectively, apparently a government experiment designed to discover how much stress one human being can absorb.	game/script.rpy	53	y "[what]"
start_88a88360		Your phone vibrates again. A message from Person A appears.	game/script.rpy	55	"[what]"
start_deac6bd0	a	Hey! Have you got a minute?	game/script.rpy	57	a "[what]"
start_0e856fca	y	That question has never once meant one minute.	game/script.rpy	59	y "[what]"
start_e370c30e	y	Hey. What's up?	game/script.rpy	61	y "[what]"
start_0381fc0b	a	Sorry to message so late. I know everyone's busy, but I've run into a problem with the charity's funding proposal.	game/script.rpy	63	a "[what]"
start_64b23791	a	The draft needs reviewing before the board meeting on Friday. I thought I had most of it covered, but some of the figures don't match the latest budget.	game/script.rpy	65	a "[what]"
start_c68adb90	y	How much needs doing?	game/script.rpy	67	y "[what]"
start_92ef2fec	a	The figures need checking, the impact section needs updating, and we need someone to review the final version. I was hoping you could help me get it sorted by Wednesday evening.	game/script.rpy	69	a "[what]"
start_a531b72a	y	Wednesday evening.	game/script.rpy	71	y "[what]"
start_15d1306c	y	The same Wednesday evening that my university assignment is due. The assignment I have been working on for three weeks. The assignment that currently consists of an introduction, two paragraphs and a deeply optimistic conclusion.	game/script.rpy	73	y "[what]"
start_e226af98	y	I might be able to help. How much work do you think it is?	game/script.rpy	75	y "[what]"
start_b067314f	a	A few hours, probably. Maybe four if the figures are a mess.	game/script.rpy	77	a "[what]"
start_365b01be	y	Four hours. That's almost a whole evening, assuming time has stopped existing for food, sleep and basic hygiene.	game/script.rpy	79	y "[what]"
start_91efc035	a	I wouldn't ask if it wasn't important. The funding could make a real difference to the people we support.	game/script.rpy	81	a "[what]"
start_b30ee685	y	And that's the difficult part. This isn't meaningless admin. The proposal matters. People are relying on us to make sensible decisions and use the charity's resources properly.	game/script.rpy	83	y "[what]"
start_6aa7b492	y	But my other commitments matter too. I do not want to become a person who survives exclusively on caffeine and calendar notifications.	game/script.rpy	85	y "[what]"
start_689b99b4		You pause before replying.	game/script.rpy	87	"[what]"
start_aa55d10c	y	Sure. Send everything over. I'll get it done by Wednesday.	game/script.rpy	97	y "[what]"
start_33e7f135	a	Really? Thank you. That takes a massive weight off my shoulders.	game/script.rpy	99	a "[what]"
start_9072a2cd	y	They sound relieved. That should feel good. So why does my stomach feel like it has just received an eviction notice?	game/script.rpy	101	y "[what]"
start_af75d1da	y	I've agreed without checking what I can realistically deliver. I might pull it off, but I've made a promise before understanding the full cost.	game/script.rpy	103	y "[what]"
start_03bedde7	y	I want to help, but I've got an assignment due Wednesday evening. I can't promise to complete four hours of work by then.	game/script.rpy	110	y "[what]"
start_81d8336a	y	Could we identify which parts of the proposal are most urgent? I might be able to review a specific section or help find another person to share the work.	game/script.rpy	112	y "[what]"
start_c5f82f0a	a	That's fair. I hadn't realised your deadline was that close.	game/script.rpy	114	a "[what]"
start_c135123f	y	It's uncomfortable to say no to part of a request. But explaining what I can do is more useful than making a promise I might break.	game/script.rpy	116	y "[what]"
start_cccf2f0a	y	I'm busy with my own deadlines. You'll need to find someone else.	game/script.rpy	123	y "[what]"
start_53d8d4a9	a	Oh. Right. Okay.	game/script.rpy	125	a "[what]"
start_64c9e965	y	My time is limited, and I'm allowed to protect it. But I haven't asked what support is available, or whether there's a smaller task I could reasonably take on.	game/script.rpy	127	y "[what]"
start_94d1b9c9	y	A boundary doesn't have to be a slammed door. Although, admittedly, a slammed door would be quite satisfying right now.	game/script.rpy	129	y "[what]"
start_64cd6031		Tuesday, 12:35 PM.	game/script.rpy	131	"[what]"
start_f270c7e8		You are between commitments when Person A calls.	game/script.rpy	133	"[what]"
start_ce538d6f	a	Have you had a chance to look at the proposal?	game/script.rpy	135	a "[what]"
start_06e1b6b9	y	Not properly yet. What's happened?	game/script.rpy	137	y "[what]"
start_104a6ab6	a	I checked the budget again this morning. The figures are more complicated than I thought, and the person who usually handles them is unavailable until Thursday.	game/script.rpy	139	a "[what]"
start_72848ab4	a	I'm worried we'll have to present something incomplete at the meeting.	game/script.rpy	141	a "[what]"
start_2c29ccd5	y	There it is. The problem has grown while I've been trying to keep everything else moving.	game/script.rpy	143	y "[what]"
start_7aebe68c	y	I could ignore the call and protect my afternoon. I could agree to take over the whole proposal. Or I could help establish what is actually possible before anyone makes another promise.	game/script.rpy	145	y "[what]"
start_a332b919	y	Have you spoken to the others about the missing figures?	game/script.rpy	147	y "[what]"
start_8a449eb9	a	Not yet. I didn't want to worry everyone before I had a solution.	game/script.rpy	149	a "[what]"
start_2df99978	y	I can see why you'd want to fix it first. But if the deadline is at risk, the others need to know early enough to help.	game/script.rpy	151	y "[what]"
start_acda062b	a	I know. I just don't want people thinking I've failed to prepare.	game/script.rpy	153	a "[what]"
start_ef0a36ad	y	That sounds familiar. I sometimes treat asking for help as evidence that I should have been able to do everything myself.	game/script.rpy	155	y "[what]"
start_94d9c5be	y	It is a particularly inefficient habit. Unfortunately, humans are not renowned for being efficient about their insecurities.	game/script.rpy	157	y "[what]"
start_3a5bd69b	a	What do you think we should do?	game/script.rpy	159	a "[what]"
start_b15342ad	y	I'll take over the proposal and try to get it finished. We can discuss the details later.	game/script.rpy	169	y "[what]"
start_85c77077	a	Are you sure? I really appreciate it.	game/script.rpy	171	a "[what]"
start_17f6be4b	y	I'm trying to help, but I may have replaced one person's problem with two people's problems. If I cannot finish the work, the charity still has a problem, and now my other commitments are at risk too.	game/script.rpy	173	y "[what]"
start_44884a1a	y	Let's list what actually needs to be finished before Friday and what can wait. We should also let the others know that the budget figures are unresolved.	game/script.rpy	180	y "[what]"
start_847cdb4b	y	That gives us a chance to divide the work properly instead of hoping someone can absorb it all.	game/script.rpy	182	y "[what]"
start_2cde22a5	a	I was worried they'd think I'd handled it badly.	game/script.rpy	184	a "[what]"
start_c6d5fc2c	y	They might have questions, but giving them time to help is better than telling them at the last minute.	game/script.rpy	186	y "[what]"
start_8296d2da	a	Okay. I'll send a message to the group and explain what's missing.	game/script.rpy	188	a "[what]"
start_16b7fa32	y	This feels more constructive. I'm not abandoning the task, but I'm not pretending I can solve everything alone either.	game/script.rpy	190	y "[what]"
start_ce67bf74	y	We've all got deadlines. We just need to push through and get it done.	game/script.rpy	197	y "[what]"
start_3b277fee	a	Right. I'll keep working on it.	game/script.rpy	199	a "[what]"
start_a2087028	y	I've offered a slogan instead of a solution. Very efficient if the goal is to make someone feel worse without changing anything.	game/script.rpy	201	y "[what]"
start_206c1b57		Tuesday, 8:10 PM.	game/script.rpy	203	"[what]"
start_ba7693d6		You return to your laptop. Your assignment is still unfinished, and a work email has arrived asking for revisions to a report due Thursday.	game/script.rpy	205	"[what]"
start_550a7d07	y	I have approximately two hours before I need to stop working if I want a reasonable amount of sleep.	game/script.rpy	207	y "[what]"
start_caa18603	y	I could spend those hours on the assignment, the charity proposal, or my work report. There is no option where I magically become three people.	game/script.rpy	209	y "[what]"
start_e30e58f2		Another message appears from Person A.	game/script.rpy	211	"[what]"
start_fd2f0b7d	a	I've updated the group. One person can check the figures tomorrow, and another can review the impact section. Could you look over the final document on Thursday evening?	game/script.rpy	213	a "[what]"
start_186b15d2	y	Thursday evening. My work report is due that day. I don't know how long the revisions will take.	game/script.rpy	215	y "[what]"
start_9066b380	y	Thanks for organising that. Thursday evening might be difficult because of my work deadline. Can we agree on a time after I know how much revision is needed?	game/script.rpy	217	y "[what]"
start_a2f1ca30	a	Of course. Would it help if I sent you the current draft now, so you can see what needs reviewing?	game/script.rpy	219	a "[what]"
start_6a76cee4	y	Yes, as long as we're clear that I haven't committed to reviewing the whole document by a fixed time yet.	game/script.rpy	221	y "[what]"
start_8e1acf4c	a	That's fair. I'll send it over.	game/script.rpy	223	a "[what]"
start_ad225e01	y	I'm noticing something. Saying what I can and cannot do doesn't automatically make people angry. Sometimes it gives them the information they need to plan around me.	game/script.rpy	225	y "[what]"
start_51c0d608	y	Who knew that communicating clearly could be more effective than silently panicking? Someone probably wrote an entire book about it.	game/script.rpy	227	y "[what]"
start_ab819913		Wednesday, 6:20 PM.	game/script.rpy	229	"[what]"
start_0c17cef4		You submit your university assignment with eleven minutes to spare. You do not celebrate. You simply close the laptop and stare at the wall for a moment.	game/script.rpy	231	"[what]"
start_20a2684e	y	Submitted. Not perfect, perhaps, but submitted. A rare victory for the concept of finishing things.	game/script.rpy	233	y "[what]"
start_00346626		Your phone rings. It is Person A.	game/script.rpy	235	"[what]"
start_cc95da2e	a	How did the assignment go?	game/script.rpy	237	a "[what]"
start_940e47cf	y	It's submitted. I'm relieved, but I've still got my work report to finish.	game/script.rpy	239	y "[what]"
start_1f94600e	a	I'm glad you got it done. The others have checked the figures, and the proposal is nearly ready. We still need someone to check the final version.	game/script.rpy	241	a "[what]"
start_3778d0c9	a	Would you have time tomorrow, if I send it over in the morning?	game/script.rpy	243	a "[what]"
start_6d9185d7	y	I want to help. I also know that saying yes now could create another deadline I cannot meet. The final document might need a quick proofread, or it might need another four hours of work.	game/script.rpy	245	y "[what]"
start_4ffbb589	y	Being responsible means taking the charity seriously. It also means being honest about the quality and amount of work I can deliver.	game/script.rpy	247	y "[what]"
start_6b07a158	y	Yes, I'll review the whole thing tomorrow. Send it over and I'll fit it in.	game/script.rpy	257	y "[what]"
start_e03980c4	a	Brilliant. Thank you.	game/script.rpy	259	a "[what]"
start_f2b050e7	y	I have made another promise without checking the actual workload. I might manage it, but I have left no room for unexpected problems.	game/script.rpy	261	y "[what]"
start_d2f7a847	y	I can set aside an hour tomorrow evening after my work report is submitted. If you send the document in the morning, I'll focus on checking the sections that still need attention.	game/script.rpy	268	y "[what]"
start_b03c55fe	y	If it needs more than that, we'll need to agree who can do the remaining work or whether the deadline needs to be reconsidered.	game/script.rpy	270	y "[what]"
start_d0f6146f	a	That works. I'll flag the sections that need the most attention.	game/script.rpy	272	a "[what]"
start_151a30f0	y	Good. That way, we know what I can realistically contribute, and you can plan around it.	game/script.rpy	274	y "[what]"
start_e7e412b9	a	Thanks. I appreciate you being clear about it.	game/script.rpy	276	a "[what]"
start_28a82ac0	y	A specific offer is more useful than a vague promise. It gives Person A something they can actually plan around.	game/script.rpy	278	y "[what]"
start_bea169b1	y	I can't do it. I've got too much going on.	game/script.rpy	285	y "[what]"
start_4b0f7fb6	a	Okay. I'll see if anyone else can help.	game/script.rpy	287	a "[what]"
start_acb79e52	y	I may genuinely need to step back. But I haven't explained what I can offer, helped identify the remaining work, or checked whether the plan is still realistic.	game/script.rpy	289	y "[what]"
start_57f46021		Thursday, 7:15 PM.	game/script.rpy	291	"[what]"
start_942b689a		Your work report has been submitted. Your inbox is finally quiet.	game/script.rpy	293	"[what]"
start_b537a4f4		You open the charity's latest message. The proposal is ready for the Friday meeting, and Person A has thanked everyone who contributed.	game/script.rpy	295	"[what]"
start_0c417cf2	a	We've got the proposal ready. Thanks for helping us work through it.	game/script.rpy	297	a "[what]"
start_e545a14a	y	I'm glad we got it sorted.	game/script.rpy	299	y "[what]"
start_f49f5479	a	I think I learned something from this week. I assumed I had to solve the problem before telling anyone about it.	game/script.rpy	301	a "[what]"
start_c527c99b	y	I can relate to that. I sometimes say yes because I don't want to disappoint people, even when I haven't worked out whether I can deliver.	game/script.rpy	303	y "[what]"
start_d9534b4d	a	It's difficult to balance everything. The charity matters, but so do the people doing the work.	game/script.rpy	305	a "[what]"
start_66ff0467	y	That's probably the point I needed to remember. A charity doesn't benefit from staff who exhaust themselves trying to prove they're committed.	game/script.rpy	307	y "[what]"
start_28d1bc57	y	But boundaries aren't an excuse to disengage, either. Other people depend on us, and we need to communicate, share responsibility and make decisions that protect the work we're here to do.	game/script.rpy	309	y "[what]"
start_f00cced0	a	Next time, I think I'll raise the problem earlier and ask for help before the deadline becomes urgent.	game/script.rpy	311	a "[what]"
start_9e96bb13	y	And I'll check my actual capacity before agreeing to anything. At least, that's the plan.	game/script.rpy	313	y "[what]"
start_0be560ff	a	A plan is a start.	game/script.rpy	315	a "[what]"
start_1df615e0	y	A plan, a calendar, and possibly a second mug of tea. The foundations of responsible leadership.	game/script.rpy	317	y "[what]"
start_b3e0a46e		Your decisions have shaped how you managed your commitments, communicated with others, and supported the charity.	game/script.rpy	320	"[what]"
start_9cb65341		Based on your chosen responses, you may have a tendency to overcommit without realising.	game/script.rpy	323	"[what]"
start_80dc1c17		Advice: Before committing to a task, assess your existing workload and be honest about your availability.	game/script.rpy	325	"[what]"
start_50bef7ad		Setting boundaries is not selfish. It helps you make commitments you can actually fulfil.	game/script.rpy	327	"[what]"
start_d52aa5d2		Based on your chosen responses, you showed some awareness of your limits.	game/script.rpy	330	"[what]"
start_efe7b4a3		But, your decisions did not consistently demonstrate effective boundary-setting.	game/script.rpy	332	"[what]"
start_c3bc5e0d		Advice: Practise distinguishing between what you would like to do and what you can realistically do.	game/script.rpy	334	"[what]"
start_8712cb7d		Offer specific, manageable contributions rather than making open-ended promises.	game/script.rpy	336	"[what]"
start_520a3276		You demonstrated an ability to recognise your limits and set realistic expectations around your commitments.	game/script.rpy	339	"[what]"
start_2007d084		Advice: Continue communicating your capacity early.	game/script.rpy	341	"[what]"
start_32924c82		Remember that setting a boundary works best when you also help identify a practical way forward.	game/script.rpy	343	"[what]"
start_84f6469f		Based on your chosen responses, you may not always be fully clear to other people.	game/script.rpy	346	"[what]"
start_ccf3b88d		Your decisions may have left others uncertain about your availability, priorities, or the support you could provide.	game/script.rpy	348	"[what]"
start_d4cf6be9		Advice: Communicate early and directly when difficulties arise.	game/script.rpy	350	"[what]"
